/**
 * The Client info card. OB Admin, Onboarding Manager, and Sales for a
client they created.

**Purchases are not editable here.** Adding a product is buying one —
it instantiates a journey — and removing one would orphan a journey
that may already have run. Both are their own operations under
`/onboarding/clients/{obClientId}/applications`, landing with the
journey-engine slice.

`pan` is immutable once set. It is the duplicate guard's key and an
identity fact, not a correctable typo: a wrong PAN is a wrong client
row, and the honest repair is `status: DROPPED` and a new one.

`status` is here rather than on its own setter because three of the
four values are a judgement somebody records with a reason —
`ON_HOLD`, `DROPPED`, and back — while the fourth, `LIVE`, **cannot be
set at all**: it is the go-live flip, earned when every journey
completes with its sign-offs (plan §5.9), and `422` if asked for
directly. A status a person can type is a status that disagrees with
the journeys underneath it.

 * @summary Edit client info (OB-05)
 */
export const updateObClientParams = zod.object({
  "obClientId": zod.number().describe('A-118 · an `ob_clients` id, \*\*not\*\* a ticketing `clients` id. The two\nmasters are disjoint tables and the ids do not correspond; a client\npresent in both is joined at the identity layer by an explicit audited\nlink, never by a shared key.\n')
})

